{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01QbgZ1ukevJSq5ZKd88UQpA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Written and committed **`06-004-optimize-for-latency-or-throughput-personal-assistant-one-re.md`**.\n\n**What's in the note:**\n\n- **Framing:** Probes whether you understand the latency-vs-throughput tension and can apply it immediately to a concrete scenario (single interactive user = latency first, not throughput).\n- **Answer (Concept → Mechanism → Example):**\n  - The core insight: batching is the primary throughput lever *and* the primary latency killer — batch size = 1 is correct for a solo interactive assistant\n  - Four latency levers: streaming SSE (TTFT perceived fix), model tiering (GPT-4o-mini → 150ms vs GPT-4o → 400ms TTFT), semantic caching in Redis (cosine > 0.93, ~10ms on cache hit), and LLMLingua prompt compression (30–50% prefill token reduction)\n  - When the objective flips: multi-tenant API → maximize throughput via PagedAttention, large `--max-num-seqs`, high batch utilization\n- **Pitfalls:** Conflating \"one request\" with \"needs throughput optimization\"; defining latency abstractly without naming levers; treating \"faster GPU\" as the answer when model selection and caching dwarf hardware differences\n- **Cross-links:** Q5 (data parallelism — the direct follow-up), Q11 (§7 broader latency catalogue), Q3 (diagnostic mindset)"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":290,"cache_read_input_tokens":72881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":290,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd0cb2d7-db5f-4dcf-8f87-b87dedc625a6","uuid":"535e27b7-5ebe-4a18-a6b9-88ad77c7b95d"}
